yesterday was the first day of spring so  it's time to talk new water conservation  rules with the southern nevada water  authority the sponsors of this next  segment  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  hi i'm jc davis i'm the manager of  enterprise conservation for the southern  nevada water authority uh we're the part  of the organization that works with  non-residential customers whether  they're commercial properties like this  one or homeowners association or  apartment complexes  and  we're focused at this moment on what's  called assembly bill 356  which is a new state law  that is going to prohibit irrigation  of non-functional turf by the end of  2026 using colorado river water  so our  our focus is in trying to help these  customers  remove that turf and replace it with  drip irrigated landscapes which use  about 75 percent less water one of the  things that we're doing to try to help  customers make the transition is we  actually offer incentive money  for them to make the replacement of the  grass  with drip irrigated landscapes so we pay  three dollars a square foot for the  first 10 000 square feet and then a  dollar fifty a square foot thereafter  with no cap so while single family  residents aren't subject to this new law  so they don't have to take out their  turf a lot of them want to and in fact  we're seeing epic numbers in terms of  participation in our water smart  landscapes rebate program so we want to  make things as easy as possible on our  customers so we have a variety of  resources available for them everything  from sample landscape designs for  residential properties to a an updated  plant list of plants that do really well  in this climate to even a list of  qualified contractors that have  completed training with us so that we  know that they're going to be able to do  the job correctly so as of march 1st the  spring watering schedule came into  effect so people can water up to three  days a week on their assigned days  but if you have a drip irrigated  landscape already you don't need to for  this time of year until the temps kind  of break into the 80s and low 90s two  days a week is just fine for a drip  landscape and so you'll save water your  plants will actually be healthier for it  if people don't know what their watering  group is and what days they're supposed  to water they can go to snwa.com  they can get information about the  watering schedule and all of the other  things that i mentioned so this year  because of the colorado river drought  we're taking a seven billion gallon  shortage to our already small allocation  and so we can't afford to use water on  purely non-functional grass and things  like that um you know our focus is not  to reduce anybody's quality of life or  drive customers away from people's  businesses but rather to just make sure  we're making the best use of the water  we have  thank you again to southern nevada water  authority for sponsoring this segment  for more information call 702 258 save  or visit snwa.com
 

